! Custody per face, 2026 ~ who seals, under whose key, and what no vessel holds

F6 moves the browser's Repo and daemon kernel into its shared holder, and the kernel's sealed writes asked where the key lives. Four ratcheted research rounds (2026-10-19) answered, each a Luna-6 YIN and YANG pair in two traded waves, YIN with live search over vendor and spec pages, YANG over the code: round one on the fork, round two on per-face custody keys, round three on the question the operator answered Mu, round four confirming the model across every vessel type. The rulings rest at #/rulings; the eight wave-two handbacks rest verbatim at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">>. Rows `K15`, `F6`, `B1` and `X0` of <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/stable-founding-road">> carry the build.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/rulings>>

!! The rulings (operator, 2026-10-19)

# ''The asker seals.'' The browser holder routes each sealed write to the admitted tab whose session caused it, else to any admitted, unlocked tab of that face; with no awake admitted tab the write waits, named `seal-awaits-session`. A SharedWorker carries no promise to run while its tabs freeze, and no `MessagePort` event reports a peer's death, so a wait names itself and no clock decides it.
# ''A face's custody rows seal under its own key.'' Each PersonaGroup holds an independent random face custody key (FCK) that only its own slot tree opens, never wrapped under a vessel key; a holder of the VK or of a sibling's unlock opens none of its rows.
# ''A shared act never makes a shared secret.'' Every act touching more than one face sorts D (its private bytes dissolve into the owning face's rows), F (its public bytes stand at the floor by design) or S (a named vessel secret, its principal and opener named). The browser holds no vessel key; a node holds S only for rows it names.

!! What each vessel holds

|! vessel |! its S set |! how a face unlocks |! what its floor and its last transition hold |
| desktop browser tab | none | each tab asks its own face | the last tab drops every live FCK and rests at the floor |
| iOS and Android leaves, installed web app or WebAPK | none | each tab or app session asks its face | app loss returns to the floor; installing grants no key |
| tablet as waking floor | none | a foreground face session asks its face | unattended, it carries ciphertext and public rows and decrypts no face's row |
| probe, CLI, MCP and watcher seats | none | each run or session presents its own face at the node's gate | a seat's exit holds no key |
| native or desktop shell (planned) | none | each shell session asks its face | an OS keychain may unwrap a face's FCK and never owns a row |
| guest island (planned) | none, unless it owns a key store, and then it stands as its own vessel | a host-admitted face | the guest's end drops its capability |
| node daemon and hearth | the serving key's rows once they exist (none today), and the open place signer | the node unlocks once per boot for its S; each face proves itself apart | restart drops every volatile face and serving key |
| herm | the open place signer | no face sits (`personaSlotCeiling("herm") === 0`) | it serves its public floor before any face; a reboot drops a raise |
| container vessels | the node's set | an attached hand unlocks after each restart | an unattended restart stands at the floor and unlocks nothing |

!! Open joints

* ''The place signer.'' A node's private place signing seed rests clear and loads at boot, so a herm serves its floor with no face (`node-vessel-identity.ts:429-442`). Either it stands as a named floor signer protected at rest, the floor then holding no custody key rather than no key, or it moves behind a boot custody door and a herm's unattended floor waits for a hand.
* ''The node archive selector.'' Which veil row the node writes rests unproven against its writer chain; K15's step 0 proves it before bytes move.
* ''Unattended private work.'' A tablet or herm that must decrypt a face's row while no face sits would need a named S of its own; the law grants none.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-place-signer>>

!! The place signer (a fifth round, 2026-10-19)

A YANG scout read the code and a YIN scout read the field, then they traded. The node's place seed rests clear as hex JSON, mode `0600`, and loads at boot with no hand; it signs the place KEL's inception, `carry-for` seals and floor pins, carriage presentations, the floor card and the floor-gate children (through a domain-registered door that never hands out the seed), and it reaches past the floor: the relay gate, the daemon's auth, internal asks, the device-key signer, `deriveDyadVeil` for every face's device veil, and `groupSecretOpenerFromSeed`. The place KEL commits no next key, so a stolen seed re-founds. SSH host keys, the Tor onion master key, WireGuard and ACME account keys load unattended under file permissions; systemd encrypted credentials with TPM2 bind release to the host and its boot state; Vault auto-unseal and Kubernetes secrets move trust to a machine identity; KERI pre-rotation alone makes a theft recoverable. Both scouts ranked a pre-rotated place KEL with a scoped online signer first, a named protected signer second, and a boot door third. The operator ruled the first, its next key riding the founding card, and ruled each face's device veil a random key of its own under that face's FCK. The four handbacks rest verbatim at <<~ loulou "lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/docs/history/spirit-handback-custody-per-face">> #/r5-yin-wave1-yin-wave-one-internet-scout-place-signer onward.

